---
// The home page: the question in large type, the figure, the count, one paragraph of
// explanation, the seven branches. Every number comes from tree.json (scripts/export.py).
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import { tree } from '../lib/tree';

const c = tree.counts;
const stages = tree.stages;
const examplesOnly = c.upgrades > 0 && c.examples === c.upgrades;
---

<Base>
  <section class="home-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">A public count, kept in the open</p>
    <h1 class="question">How much of a human being can we change on purpose today?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Not in a story, and not someday. Today, with a device, a drug or a procedure that at least one
      person has actually had. This site keeps count, and watches the one change that would matter
      most if it came: a direct link between a brain and a machine.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="home-grid">
    <div class="figure-card">
      <Figure />
      <ul class="legend">
        <li><i class="stage-none"></i> nothing recorded yet</li>
        {stages.map((s) => <li><i class={`stage-${s.rank}`}></i> {s.label.toLowerCase()}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p class="figure-note">Each part of the figure is one of the seven branches, shaded by the furthest stage any upgrade under it has reached. Click a part to see what is there.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="headline">
      <div class="count-card">
        <p class="big-number">{c.first_human_or_beyond}<small>of {c.upgrades}</small></p>
        <p class="big-claim">
          {c.first_human_or_beyond === 1 ? 'upgrade on the tree has' : 'upgrades on the tree have'} reached a first human.
        </p>
        <p class="big-sub">
          Counted from the record, not chosen by hand. An upgrade counts once a public record shows
          that at least one person has had it, whether or not anyone approved.
        </p>
        <div class="stage-row">
          {stages.map((s) => (
            <div>
              <span class="stat">{c.by_stage[s.key]}</span>
              <span class="stat-label">{s.label.toLowerCase()}</span>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="checked-strip">
        <span class="k">Last checked</span>
        <span>{tree.last_checked ? tree.last_checked : 'not yet checked'}</span>
        <span class="dim">A regular reading of the news against every upgrade is coming; until the first one runs, this says so.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section explain narrow">
    <p>
      An <em>upgrade</em>, here, is one specific way of changing a human being on purpose: an implant that
      gives back some sight, a pill meant to slow ageing, a chip that lets a paralysed person type by
      thought. Each one is written down with a plain test of what would count as a person having it,
      the public record that settles that, and a <em>stage</em>: idea only, shown in animals, a first
      human, in use by doctors, or in wide use. The stage is a fact about what has happened, not a
      guess about what will. There are no odds on this site. The seven <em>branches</em> are the seven
      things about a person that can be changed: what they sense, how they move, how they think and
      remember, how they feel, how long they last, what they pass to their children, and how directly
      they can be joined to a machine.
    </p>
    {examplesOnly && (
      <p class="dim" style="font-size: 15px;">
        The {c.upgrades} upgrades on the tree today are examples, seeded on 16 October 2026 to show how the
        record works while the real list is written. Each says so on its page. The counts above will change
        as the list fills in.
      </p>
    )}
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shead"><h2>The seven branches</h2><span>what is recorded under each</span></div>
    <ul class="branches">
      {tree.branches.map((b) => (
        <li>
          <a class="bcard" href={b.path}>
            <div class="bcard-top"><span class="n">{b.order} of 7</span><span>{b.count === 0 ? 'nothing yet' : `${b.count} upgrade${b.count === 1 ? '' : 's'}`}</span></div>
            <div class="bcard-title">{b.name}</div>
            <div class="bcard-sub">{b.tagline}</div>
            <div class="bcard-foot">{b.furthest_label ? `Furthest stage: ${b.furthest_label.toLowerCase()}` : 'No stage reached'}</div>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section narrow prose">
    <h2>Why keep count</h2>
    <p>
      One idea about the future says that as machines get smarter, people keep up by joining with
      them: better senses, better memory, a direct line from brain to computer. Whether that is
      happening is a question with an answer, and the answer is a list. This site is that list,
      kept in the open so that anyone can check an entry, argue with a stage, or add what is missing.
      The rules for deciding a stage are on the <a href="/method/">Method</a> page, every change is
      dated on the <a href="/story/">Story</a> page, and the place to push back is
      <a href="/disagree/">Disagree with something</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
